.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The search uses \f[CR]strstr(3)\f[R] to find \f[CR]\(dqkey\(dq\f[R] in
the JSON string.
This means a key could theoretically match inside a string value if the
value contains the same pattern.
For the bash\-server protocol\(cqs limited vocabulary, this is not a
practical concern.
.IP \(bu 2
The key search buffer is limited to 256 bytes (including quotes).
Keys longer than 253 characters will not be found.
.IP \(bu 2
Unicode escape sequences (\f[CR]\(rsuXXXX\f[R]) are only decoded for
code points in the ASCII range (< 0x80).
Non\-ASCII code points are silently skipped.
.IP \(bu 2
The function skips whitespace and colons between the key and value,
tolerating variations in JSON formatting.
.IP \(bu 2
This is not a general\-purpose JSON parser.
It does not handle nested objects, arrays, or values that are not
strings.
Use \f[CR]json_get_int(3)\f[R] for integer values.
